<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <section xml:id="sec-section-1-4">
      <title>Section 04 - Introduction to Three-Dimensional Coordinate Systems</title>

      <objectives>
  <p><em>Learning Objectives</em></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Sketch a point in three-dimensional space.</li>
    <li>Find the distance between two points in space.</li>
  </ul>
</objectives>

      <subsection xml:id="sec-04-01-3d-system">
        <title>The Three-Dimensional Coordinate System</title>
        <p>In the Three-Dimensional Coordinate System, instead of quadrants,
        we now have octants, designated by cubes in <xref ref="octants"/> below.</p>
<figure xml:id="octants">
<title>Vectors with initial and terminal points</title>
  <image source="images/ch01/Octants.png">
    <shortdescription>
Diagrams showing the eight octants in three-dimensional space labeled
Octants I through VIII relative to the coordinate axes.
    </shortdescription>
  </image>
  <caption>
    Octants in the Three-Dimensional Coordinate System
  </caption>
</figure>

<p>
  The orientation of the coordinate axes in three-dimensional space depends
  on whether a right-handed or left-handed system is used. <xref ref="coordinate-systems"/> below
  illustrates how the positive <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m> directions are
  determined in each case.
</p>
<figure xml:id="coordinate-systems">
  <title>Right-Handed and Left-Handed Coordinate Systems</title>

  <image source="CoordinateSystems.png">
    <shortdescription>
      Diagrams showing how the x, y, and z axes are oriented in right-handed
      and left-handed coordinate systems.
    </shortdescription>
  </image>

  <caption>
    Orientation of the coordinate axes in right-handed and left-handed
    three-dimensional coordinate systems.
  </caption>
</figure>

<table xml:id="tab-handed-coordinate-systems">
  <title>Right-Handed and Left-Handed Coordinate Systems</title>

  <tabular>
    <row header="yes">
      <cell>Right-Handed System</cell>
      <cell>Left-Handed System</cell>
    </row>

    <row>
      <cell>
        <p>
          This is the system used in calculus, physics, engineering, and most
          textbooks.
        </p>

        <p>
          In the diagram:
        </p>

        <p>
          Thumb → positive <m>x</m><br/>
          Index finger → positive <m>y</m><br/>
          Middle finger → positive <m>z</m>
        </p>

        <p>
          These three fingers form an L-shape with the middle finger pointing
          upward. This depiction is correct.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Key property:</em> Curling the fingers of your right hand from the
          <m>x</m>-axis toward the <m>y</m>-axis causes your thumb to point along
          the positive <m>z</m>-direction.
        </p>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <p>
          This system is less common, but it is used in some computer graphics
          and animation settings.
        </p>

        <p>
          In the diagram:
        </p>

        <p>
          Thumb → positive <m>x</m><br/>
          Index finger → positive <m>y</m><br/>
          Middle finger (or palm direction) → positive <m>z</m>, but downward
          instead of upward
        </p>

        <p>
          This depiction matches the definition of a left-handed coordinate
          system.
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Key property:</em> Curling the fingers of your left hand from
          <m>x</m> toward <m>y</m> causes your thumb to point along the
          negative <m>z</m>-direction, meaning positive <m>z</m> points the
          opposite way compared to the right-handed case.
        </p>
      </cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>
</table>
      </subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-01-07-3d-coordinate-applet">
  <title>Using a 3D Coordinate Applet</title>

  <handout>
    <title>Concept Check</title>

    <checkpoint knowl="no">
      <statement>

        <p>
          We will use an interactive applet to visualize points in
          three-dimensional space.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Plot the point <m>(1, 2, 4)</m>.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Identify which octant the point lies in.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Describe how each coordinate affects the point’s position.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Now plot the point <m>(-1, 0, 2)</m>.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Which coordinate causes the point to lie on a coordinate
                  plane?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Which plane is the point on?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Rotate the view and observe how the same point appears from
          different perspectives.
        </p>

      </statement>
    </checkpoint>
  </handout>

  <note knowl="no">
    <title>3D Coordinate Applet (Optional Exploration)</title>
<p> To explore points in three-dimensional space interactively, open the
  <url href="davidmathlogic.com//amit/pointplotwithinput.html"> 
    3D Coordinate Applet (external link) </url>. </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Plot points in three-dimensional space.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Rotate the axes to view points from different perspectives.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Identify octants and coordinate planes visually.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      This tool is especially helpful if three-dimensional sketching
      feels unfamiliar.
    </p>
  </note>
</subsection>

